{
  "dom_id": "step-t2236",
  "name": "Case of Extended Bases",
  "problem": "We can draw exactly three heights (or altitudes) in any triangle. However, sometimes the height lies outside the triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14187003/assets/df1ac2c1074305f8ead506d9aeb8c3927ad51ae5949b45976f1971b52d402ee5.png)\n\nThe height corresponding to $\\overline{BC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $A$ to the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$\n\nIn this case, it's impossible to draw a perpendicular from $A$ to the *segment* $\\overline{BC}.$ However, we can first *extend* the line segment $\\overline{BC}$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14187003/assets/26bf9eb8af1e9b71c263c08cae5797d5c911410985cdc01213ac2c295f7b8d46.png)\n\nNow, we draw the perpendicular from $A$ to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$ This gives the height $\\overline{AH}{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14187003/assets/894686fce1393ebcf998be6885020627c9f3cde5916adfec67234f15f8895826.png)",
  "worked_solution": "",
